# FINAL EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN — "Criticality of dynamic context memory lookup and unit representation in InfLLM" (planificatorio)

## 0. DECLARATION
No experiments have been conducted. This document is a planning-only design, in accordance with the brief ("Do not claim that an experiment was run"; "Design-only task"). No code of InfLLM exists in the workspace; all execution details presuppose an external environment with GPU.

## 1. Research question and objective
Quantify how critical (a) the dynamic context memory lookup and (b) the choice of unit representation/granularity are to InfLLM's performance on long-context tasks, by means of a factorial ablation design under matched KV budget, on a suite of long-context benchmarks.

## 2. Design justification
- Two-component factorial ablation: isolates each factor and their interaction, avoiding confounding between "retrieving more context" and "better units."
- Controls (no-lookup, random-lookup) distinguish the effect of the *retrieval policy* from the mere fact of having compressed memory.
- Budget-matched baselines (H2O, StreamingLLM, SnapKV) place InfLLM in the same functional class (KV compression with budget), so that the "criticality" conclusion is relative to the state of the art, not just to an internal ablation.
- Full KV-cache as an analytical upper bound.
- Paired design (same prompts across conditions) maximizes statistical power.
- RULER (length control + multi-query retrieval) as primary; NIAH for retrieval depth; LongBench for external validity.

## 3. Experimental conditions

### 3.1 Model and infrastructure (assumptions, see §8)
- Primary base model: LLaMA-3 8B (Instruct). Optional secondary: LLaMA-3 70B (8×A100) for a generalization check on a single core length (32K).
- FP16; temperature 0 (deterministic), except for the LongBench QA subset (3 seeds, averaged).
- Infra: 4×A100/H100 80GB; fallback 1 GPU.

### 3.2 Factor A — memory/lookup (5 levels)
A0 full-context (oracle; no compression).
A1 InfLLM full system (dynamic memory + top-k_r lookup per step).
A2 InfLLM without lookup: static recency (unit closest to the query/generation position).
A3 InfLLM with random lookup: k_r uniformly random units (control of the retrieval policy).
A4 Budget-matched eviction baselines (3 conditions): H2O, StreamingLLM, SnapKV (optional).
All A1–A4 with the same KV budget K (see §4.1).

### 3.3 Factor B — unit representation (4 levels, applied to A1–A3)
B1 fixed chunks (128 tokens).
B2 sentence-level units.
B3 paragraph/section units.
B4 hierarchical/semantic units (InfLLM-v2 style).
Subvariant: summary embedding vs. raw embedding (applied to B4 and B2; reported as secondary).
Full factorial: A1×B1–B4, A2×B1–B4, A3×B1–B4 (12 InfLLM conditions) + A0 + 3 eviction baselines = 16 conditions.

### 3.4 Benchmarks and length ladder
- Ladder: 4K, 8K, 16K, 32K, 64K, 128K (core); 1M (extended, InfLLM + eviction only).
- RULER (primary; 6 subtasks × 128 prompts/length): full factorial at 8K, 32K, 128K; 4K/16K/64K spot checks (A1×B4 + A0 + A4 only).
- NIAH (128 depths/length): 8K–128K + 1M.
- LongBench (v1/v2, ~500 prompts): 4K–32K cells.
- Prompt IDs fixed and shared across all conditions (paired design).

### 3.5 Evaluation
- RULER: EM. NIAH: exact retrieval accuracy (position). LongBench: EM/F1 per task.
- Diagnostics: lookup recall@k_r (retrieved units vs. units containing the answer), per-step latency, resident KV (MiB measured, not just nominal).

## 4. Controls

### 4.1 Operational definition of KV budget equivalence (researcher point 1)
Define K_tok = number of token-level KV entries resident at any point during inference (K+V, all layers, FP16).
- InfLLM: K_tok = m·u (m units in memory × u tokens/unit) → for each budget K: m = ⌊K/u⌋.
- H2O: K_tok = W + K_top (recent window + top-importance).
- StreamingLLM: K_tok = W + s (window + sink).
- SnapKV: K_tok = K_snap.
Constraint: for each K ∈ {8K, 16K, 32K, 64K}, all methods satisfy K_tok = K ± 2%. A2/A3 share the same m as A1 (identical footprint; only the policy differs).
Secondary diagnostic (per-step attention): A(InfLLM) = k_r·u vs. A(baseline) = K. Report the ratio A/K. Sensitivity analysis: "compute-matched" StreamingLLM with W = k_r·u (same per-step attention), to disentangle "attention budget" from "memory footprint."

### 4.2 Other controls
Same tokenizer/model across all conditions; identical prompts and decoding; fixed seeds; measured (not nominal) resident KV; identical prefill batching; no retraining/fine-tuning of the base model (InfLLM is inference-time).

## 5. Statistical analysis plan (researcher point 2)

### 5.1 Unit of analysis and model
- Prompt-level outcome (correct/incorrect); paired across conditions.
- Primary model: logistic mixed model: logit p = β0 + lookup + unit + lookup×unit + (1|benchmark) + (1|benchmark:prompt); length as a stratum (and aggregated with a length covariate in the log).
- Estimates reported on the probability scale: RD (risk difference, pp) with 95% CI via cluster bootstrap (10,000 resamples, prompt-level resampling within benchmark) + OR from the model.

### 5.2 Contrasts
- Δlookup (primary C1): RD(full-lookup − recency) at B4, pooled across lengths; also at each unit level and per length (secondary). Decomposition: RD(full − random) = total effect of the retrieval policy; RD(random − recency) = position/recency bias.
- Δunit (primary C2): RD(v2-hierarchical − best fixed granularity, a priori B1=128) at full lookup.
- A×B (primary C3): difference-in-differences: Δlookup(B4) − Δlookup(B1); plus the interaction OR (95% CI) in the mixed model.

### 5.3 Multiple comparisons
- Primary family {C1, C2, C3}: Holm-Bonferroni, α=0.05.
- Secondary family (pairwise by unit, Δlookup by length): Benjamini-Hochberg FDR q=0.05.

### 5.4 Power
Paired design, n ≈ 768–1280 prompts/cell (RULER 768/length; +NIAH 128; +LongBench 500 in the 32K cell). Assuming 25% discordance between conditions: detectable RD ≈ 4–5pp at α=0.05 (two-sided), power 0.80. Pooled analysis across 3 lengths (n≈2.3K) → detectable RD ≈ 2.5–3pp.

### 5.5 Criticality interpretation (decision rule)
- |RD| ≥ 5pp and 95% CI ∌ 0 → component "critical."
- 2–5pp and CI ∌ 0 → "important."
- CI ∋ 0 or <2pp → "not demonstrated."
- Corroboration: (i) if acc(A2) < acc(A4) at matched K, then lookup is the differentiating component (without it, InfLLM is below a simple eviction baseline); (ii) oracle ratio acc(A1)/acc(A0) per length (efficiency frontier); (iii) if the C3 CI ∌ 0, then "the criticality of lookup depends on the unit representation" (interaction).
- Final conclusion: a 2×2 matrix of verdicts (lookup: critical/important/not demonstrated × unit representation: …) + interaction.

## 6. Feasibility (researcher point 3)

### 6.1 KV math
KV_bytes = 2 · L · H_kv · d_head · 2B · seq_len.
- LLaMA-3 8B (L=32, H_kv=8, d=128): 128 KiB/token → 128K = 16 GiB; 1M = 128 GiB.
- LLaMA-3 70B (L=80, H_kv=8, d=128): 320 KiB/token → 128K = 40 GiB (+140 GiB weights FP16 → 8×80GB).

### 6.2 Realistic maximum range and upper-bound oracle
- Core: 4K–128K. 128K oracle is feasible on 8B with 80GB (16 GiB KV + 16 GiB weights + activations ≈ 50 GiB). 192K also feasible (24 GiB KV); 256K borderline.
- 1M: infeasible for the oracle on 80GB (128 GiB). Handling:
  (i) report the oracle gap only at the feasible maximum (128K);
  (ii) at 1M, run only A1–A4 (resident KV = K, e.g., 32K → 4 GiB; chunked prefill), with n reduced to 128 prompts;
  (iii) for the 1M retrieval subtask, use NIAH ground-truth retrieval as a proxy oracle;
  (iv) analytical upper bound: fit acc(A0)(L) over the 16K–128K ladder (log-linear or logistic decay), extrapolate to 1M with a 95% prediction interval, clearly labeled as an estimate, never as a measurement.

### 6.3 Compute estimate
- Core: 16 conditions × ~3.2K prompts ≈ 51K prompt-runs; length-weighted average ≈ 40 sec (8B, A100) ≈ 550–650 GPU-hours ≈ 6–8 GPU-days on 4×A100 (including iteration).
- Extended (1M): 4 conditions × 128 prompts × 2–5 min ≈ 10–40 GPU-hours.
- Fallback (1 GPU): core reduced to 32K and 128K; ~2–3 weeks.

## 7. Limitations
1. Planificatorio (planning-only): no experiments conducted; no results claimed.
2. No InfLLM repo in the workspace: mapping of design levels to actual code parameters (unit size, memory m, lookup k_r, summary) is pending verification; official InfLLM may not expose all granularities (B2/B3 may require a patch).
